= Social information processing — Simple =

'''Social information processing''' is a name used for several different theories about how social information affects people.

The three most important uses are not the same theory.

== 1. Workplace attitudes ==

Gerald Salancik and Jeffrey Pfeffer proposed a social information processing approach to job attitudes in 1978.<ref name="sp">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10307892/ Salancik and Pfeffer (1978), “A Social Information Processing Approach to Job Attitudes and Task Design.”]</ref>

Their idea was that people do not judge their work only from fixed personal preferences.

They also use social information such as:

* what coworkers say;
* how other people behave;
* what standards the workplace emphasizes;
* how they interpret their own earlier choices.

For example, if coworkers constantly describe a task as boring, another worker may begin noticing its repetitive features more strongly.

This does not mean social context controls everything.

Actual working conditions still matter.

== 2. Children's social situations ==

Nicki Crick and Kenneth Dodge developed a different social information-processing model for children's social adjustment.<ref name="crick">[https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.115.1.74 Crick and Dodge (1994), “A review and reformulation of social information-processing mechanisms in children's social adjustment.”]</ref>

A simplified sequence is:

# notice social cues;
# interpret what they mean;
# decide what goal matters;
# think of possible responses;
# evaluate and choose a response;
# act.

Real processing can be faster and more complicated than this list.

The stages are useful for organizing research.

== Ambiguous situations ==

Imagine a child is bumped in a hallway.

Possible interpretations include:

* it was an accident;
* it was deliberate;
* the other child was careless;
* the situation is unclear.

A person who regularly interprets ambiguous actions as hostile may be more likely to generate aggressive responses.

This tendency is called '''hostile attribution bias'''.

It increases risk; it does not guarantee aggression.

== Goals and response choices ==

Even after interpreting the situation, different goals can produce different behavior.

A child might want to:

* avoid conflict;
* protect status;
* keep a friendship;
* get revenge.

The child then considers possible actions and expected consequences.

The chosen response can still fail because actual behavior depends on skill and changing circumstances.

== 3. Online communication ==

Joseph Walther developed another Social Information Processing Theory for computer-mediated communication.<ref name="walther">[https://doi.org/10.1177/009365092019001003 Walther, J. B. (1992), “Interpersonal Effects in Computer-Mediated Interaction.”]</ref>

Early research often assumed that text communication would always be impersonal because it lacked facial expression, tone of voice, and body language.

Walther argued that people adapt.

They can use:

* word choice;
* timing;
* punctuation;
* style;
* self-disclosure;
* other textual cues.

Relationships can therefore develop online over time.

== Time matters online ==

Text channels can transmit social information more slowly than face-to-face interaction.

People may need more messages before they form a detailed impression.

Walther and Burgoon found that computer-mediated groups became more relationally positive after repeated interaction and approached face-to-face groups on several measures.<ref name="walther-burgoon">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-2958.1992.tb00295.x Walther and Burgoon (1992), “Relational Communication in Computer-Mediated Interaction.”]</ref>

This does not mean every online relationship becomes close.

Time, motivation, platform design, and communication style matter.

== Modern platforms are different ==

Today's online communication can include:

* video;
* voice;
* profiles;
* images;
* reactions;
* message history.

So a theory originally developed for mostly text-based communication has to be applied carefully to modern platforms.

== Why the three theories should not be mixed ==

They ask different questions.

{| class="wikitable"
! Theory !! Main question
|-
| Workplace SIP || How does social context shape job attitudes?
|-
| Developmental SIP || How are social cues interpreted and turned into behavior?
|-
| Online SIP || How do relationships develop through computer-mediated communication?
|}

They share a name because all involve social information.

They do not describe one universal six-step process.

== General use of the phrase ==

Sometimes “social information processing” is used more broadly for how people process information about other people and social situations.

That broad use overlaps with [[Cognition]], [[Perception]], [[Behavior]], and [[Theory of mind]].

Researchers should state which meaning they intend.

== Artificial intelligence ==

AI systems can classify social cues and generate socially appropriate text.

That can reproduce parts of a social-information-processing task.

It does not prove that the AI has human social understanding or subjective experience.

